// **The first non-chain body.** A 2x2 rigid block that translates as a
// unit, against the ant's one-cell-wide following chain -- and the entire
// difference is `body: Rigid(...)` below. No code anywhere knows what a
// beetle is.
//
// It eats ants, and that still needs no mechanism of its own -- only the
// spelling changed at S5. It used to be `food: ["ant", "corpse"]`, a list
// of material names against a material called "ant"; it is now a carnivore
// gut (`traits` below) against `ant.ron`'s `food_class: 1.0`, which reaches
// the same two foods and no plant. Predation survives the `eats_kin`
// default because a beetle's kin is a beetle: an ant is somebody else's
// flesh. Losing a cell to a bite is handled by `creature::reconcile_chain`
// -- an ant that loses its head dies, one that loses a segment is injured.
//
// `dig_force: 0.3` is below soil's `penetration_resistance` of 0.8, so a
// beetle cannot dig. An ant can (1.0). That asymmetry plus the width is
// what makes a burrow a refuge, and neither is a rule about hiding.
(
    name: "beetle",
    cell_types: [
        (Head, []),
        (Segment, []),
    ],
    creature: Some((
        // Offsets from the head, authored facing east; `(0, 0)` is implicit.
        // y grows downward, so a negative dy is *up*: these three complete a
        // 2x2 with the head at its **bottom-front** corner. Bottom matters --
        // the head is the cell that senses and decides, so it belongs where
        // it touches the ground, the same place an ant's head is. Authored
        // the other way up the body extended into the floor and the species
        // simply could not be placed anywhere.
        body: Rigid([(-1, 0), (0, -1), (-1, -1)]),
        tick_interval: 8,
        start_energy: 1600.0,
        // **Per body cell** (`organism.rs`'s `idle_cost_per_cell`),
        // re-derived 2026-08-30 to leave this animal's bill exactly where it
        // was: 0.0375 x 4 cells = the 0.15 authored here before, and 0.10 x 4
        // = the 0.4. A beetle is a different species from an ant rather than
        // an ant at another size, so its *total* is what carries meaning and
        // is what was preserved.
        //
        // That leaves it cheaper per cell than an ant (0.0375 against 0.05),
        // which is inherited from the two species' authored totals rather
        // than chosen. It happens to point the way real metabolism scales --
        // bigger animals cost less per unit mass -- but nothing here measured
        // that, and it should not be read as a model.
        idle_cost_per_cell: 0.0375,
        move_cost_per_cell: 0.10,
        // Per active synapse per tick, as a fraction of start_energy (see
        // `CreatureDef::synapse_fraction`). 1.2500000821e-6 * 1600 is the
        // 0.002 this was authored as, *exactly* in f32 -- the obvious
        // literal 0.00000125 is not: it lands on 0.0019999999 and the
        // difference is enough to move a chaotic run. Full precision on
        // purpose.
        synapse_fraction: 0.0000012500000821091817,
        // Bigger animal, richer meat, and nothing eats a beetle yet --
        // this is the value a future predator or scavenger will read, set
        // proportionally to the ant's rather than measured, and labelled as
        // such.
        body_energy: 200.0,
        // See `ant.ron` for the derivation; this variant differs only in body
        // plan, so it carries the same stomach.
        crop_capacity: 1440.0,
        digest_rate: 3.3,
        // Pure carnivore: both foods on this menu sit at `food_class: 1.0`,
        // so `+1.0` is the faithful translation and it costs the beetle
        // nothing -- the filter reads 1.0 against flesh, which is the same
        // mouthful it took before. It also makes the beetle blind to every
        // plant food in the world for free, which the name-list was doing
        // by omission.
        traits: (1.0, 1.0, 0.0),
        trait_variance: (0.15, 0.15, 0.15),

        nest: "nest",
        dig_force: 0.3,
        nest_memory: 3000,
        // **The eye, and it is the whole of what this animal was missing.**
        // 64 cells, all-round, sized before it was built by
        // `Reports/creature-vision-sizing-2026-08-30.md` -- see
        // `organism.rs`'s `CreatureDef::sight_range`. The ant does not
        // author this field and so has no eyes at all, which is what keeps
        // the cost off every species but this one.
        //
        // A beetle could already kill: the pre-flight's own control put a
        // hungry beetle beside an ant and the ant went 24 -> 22 -> 21 cells.
        // What it could not do was *find* one, and the two lines below are
        // what turn a sighting into a pursuit.
        sight_range: 64,
        // **The price of that range, which was missing.** `sight_fraction`
        // landed 2026-08-31 to stop `sight_range` being a ratchet, and then
        // no species authored one -- so the only eye in the tree was free,
        // a reader with no writer
        // (`Reports/creature-genome-flexibility-2026-09-02.md` §6, gap 1).
        // Leaving it free while the ant pays would also make any later
        // predator/prey comparison a comparison of who was billed.
        //
        // Same derivation as `ant.ron`'s, at this animal's own constants:
        //
        //   idle ticks = start_energy / (idle_cost_per_cell * cells)
        //              = 1600 / (0.0375 * 4) = 10,666.7
        //   one cast at reach 64 on bare open floor reads 1,186 cells
        //     (`field_sense_probe mode=control eye=64`)
        //   S = 0.10 of an idle lifetime, matching the synapse tax:
        //   0.10 / (10,666.7 * 1,186) = 7.9047e-9
        //
        // A beetle's eye is therefore *cheaper per cell* than an ant's, and
        // that is not a thumb on the scale: it is a much larger animal with
        // a much longer life, so the same share of it buys more looking.
        sight_fraction: 0.0000000079047218,
        sensor_offset: 6,
        instincts: [
            (Bias, Move, 2.0),
            (FoodAdjacent, Move, -2.0),
            (FoodAdjacent, Dig, 2.0),
            // The predator's whole verb, and before the Feed/Dig split it
            // rode on the line above: a beetle "digs" an ant out of the
            // world. Same weight, so it hunts exactly as it did.
            (FoodAdjacent, Feed, 2.0),
            (TempAboveAmb, Turn, -0.8),
            // **Steer at what it can see.** `PreyBearing` is positive to
            // the right and `Turn` biases *left* when positive, so pursuit
            // is a negative weight -- the same sign `(PheroALateral, Turn,
            // ..)` carries in `ant.ron` for the same reason.
            (PreyBearing, Turn, -2.5),
            // **`(PreyNear, Persist, w)` is measured, better on the
            // field, worse in a corridor, and deliberately NOT shipped.**
            // Releasing straight-ahead persistence in proportion to how
            // near the prey is lets the turn win against a `Persist`
            // default that otherwise matches it, and over 8 seeds it is
            // the strongest lever in the sweep: mean sighted range 12.5 ->
            // 12.2 -> 9.1 and prey caught 323 -> 345 -> 355 at w = 0, -3,
            // -6. In a sealed 20-cell chamber it cost the head-bite 6/8 ->
            // 4/8 at the same total of one catch per run, which is a
            // chaotic tie-break rather than a worse predator -- and that
            // is exactly the sort of disagreement this project settles by
            // eye rather than by picking the number it likes.
            //
            // So the shipped animal turns and nothing else, the knob lives
            // in `predation_probe`'s `release=`, and the question is with
            // the owner. Full sweep in
            // `Reports/creature-sight-sense-2026-08-30.md`.
        ],
    )),
)
